  m_FontData:
    m_Font: {fileID: 12800000, guid: 5a8ef543591268943a212da7372c3294, type: 3}
    m_FontSize: 68
    m_FontStyle: 0
    m_BestFit: 0
    m_MinSize: 0
    m_MaxSize: 186
    m_Alignment: 4
    m_AlignByGeometry: 0
    m_RichText: 1
    m_HorizontalOverflow: 0
    m_VerticalOverflow: 0
    m_LineSpacing: 1
  m_Text: The Burmese Python (Python bivittatus) is an invasive species of predator
    which has quickly taken over the Everglades. Because of their large size, adult
    Burmese pythons have few predators, with humans being the exception. They prey
    upon native species and may reduce their populations locally. Severe declines
    in mammal populations throughout the Everglades have been linked to Burmese pythons,
    with the most severe declines in native species having occurred in the remote
    southernmost regions of the Everglades where pythons have been established the
    longest. As one of the largest snakes in the world, they can grow to impressive
    sizes. Adult Burmese pythons caught in Florida average between 6 and 9 feet.
    Generally their diet consists of various mammals, birds and reptiles.
